L0S shell, on an L0 microkernel. Type help for the commands.
l0s>   ls [PATH ...]   every file, or what is in each directory; programs are
                  in /bin, services in /sbin, apps in /Applications, and
                  the disk's volumes in /Volumes
  cat FILE ...    show files
  write FILE WORDS ...
                  make FILE hold WORDS, a line; append adds the line
  cp FROM TO      copy a file, into TO if it is a directory
  mv FROM TO      rename or move a file
  rm FILE ...     remove files, and directories with nothing in them
  mkdir DIR ...   make directories, on a volume
  cd [DIR]        make DIR the current directory, / if none: a path with
                  no / at the start is from it; pwd says which it is
  pwd             the current directory
  df              each volume's size, and the room left on it
  format VOLUME   empty a volume, /Volumes/LABEL or LABEL
  unmount VOLUME  take a volume away, until the disk is rescanned
  relabel VOLUME LABEL
                  rename a volume, mounting it at /Volumes/LABEL
  partition SECTORS [START]
                  a new partition on the disk; says its UUID
  mkfs UUID LABEL [fat32]
                  a new volume on that partition, at /Volumes/LABEL;
                  fat32 makes it FAT32, which a Raspberry Pi boots from
  ps              the processes running: the CPU each is on, its % of
                  one CPU, the names it serves, and what it is doing
  names           every name registered, its port, and who serves it
  uptime          how long the system has run, its load, and each
                  CPU's use over a second
  dmesg           the kernel's log: what booted and what the drivers
                  found, each line with its time since boot
  run NAME [ARG ...] [&]
                  run /bin/NAME, /sbin/NAME, or the program NAME in an
                  app; an app, by its name, as run Terminal; or the
                  program or app at NAME if it holds a /; with ARGs in
                  place of its own; & leaves it running, and
                  Ctrl-C ends one that is not
  wait PID        wait for a process to exit; Ctrl-C stops waiting
  kill PID        end a process, unless it is a driver
  sleep MS        sleep for MS milliseconds
  echo WORDS      print WORDS
  exit [STATUS]   stop the system
  reboot          restart the board, as its reset would
